# =============================================================================
# Solana Studio — environment configuration
# =============================================================================
#
# A Next.js app you can deploy to Vercel as-is. The engineering workspace runs
# entirely in the browser; the ONLY server-side code is an optional OAuth
# token-exchange route (src/app/api/github-oauth/route.ts).
#
# Your GitHub token and AI API key are entered through the UI at runtime and
# stay in your browser (sessionStorage by default; localStorage only if you opt
# in). They are NEVER read from environment variables and NEVER committed.
#
# IMPORTANT: Next inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable into the browser bundle.
# NEVER put a token, API key, or any secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable — treat
# them as public. Server-only secrets use plain (non-public) names.
#
# There are NO required environment variables. Everything below is optional.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional: override the GitHub API base URL (e.g. for GitHub Enterprise).
# Public value only. Leave unset to use https://api.github.com.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_API_BASE=https://api.github.com

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: one-click "Connect with GitHub" (OAuth).
#
# This is the ONLY feature that uses server-side code: the token-exchange route
# handler at /api/github-oauth. Leave these unset to keep the connect screen on
# the guided personal-access-token flow instead. To enable OAuth, set all three:
#
#   1) Create a GitHub OAuth App (Settings → Developer settings → OAuth Apps).
#      Homepage URL          = your deployed origin, e.g. https://your-app.vercel.app
#      Authorization callback URL = https://your-app.vercel.app/app
#      (the connect screen lives at /app, which is where GitHub redirects back).
#
#   2) client id is PUBLIC — safe to expose to the browser:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=Iv1_xxxxxxxxxxxx
#
#   3) client SECRET is server-only — a NON-public env var so it is NEVER
#      bundled into the browser. Set both of these in your Vercel project env:
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=Iv1_xxxxxxxxxxxx
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret_here
#
#   Optional: point the browser at a differently-hosted exchange endpoint.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OAUTH_ENDPOINT=/api/github-oauth
